WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.093 --> 00:00:02.300
- When self-isolation first started,

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:03.280
I was like, you know what?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.500
This is gonna be a piece of cake.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.260
I work from home. I'm
at home all the time.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.783
This should be a cakewalk.

00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:11.950
(solemn violin music)

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.670
It was a lot harder than
I thought it would be.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.070
Especially at the beginning.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.870
My sleep schedule went to crap.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.410
I stopped showering right when I got up,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.440
because I didn't really
have anywhere to be.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.290
I also started playing
a lot more video games.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.570
- Looking for movement.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.190
- Dude, my favorite thing in this game

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.390
is running people over with my car.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:36.740
- That's my favorite thing in general.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.070
So I think part of the
reason why this quarantine

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.380
was so challenging was because, I think,

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.570
there's a huge difference
between spending time at home

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.530
and not being able to leave home.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.880
So for instance, with my own work habits,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.340
yeah, I work from home,
but I also have this habit

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.660
of leaving the house whenever I'm starting

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.940
to reach a lull in my productivity

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:57.890
or when I'm kind of
goofing off a little bit,

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:58.940
I'll say, you know what?

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.880
I should hit a coffee
shop and start writing.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.380
But I don't have that option anymore.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.320
I also usually like hitting the gym,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.570
getting the blood pumping,

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.380
so that I can take your mind off things,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.930
reenergizing you for the day,
but there's no gym anymore.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:11.763
They're all closed.

00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:14.040
Another huge barrier that
I've been encountering

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.655
is that I share this house
with three other guys

00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:18.270
who I'm quarantining with.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.030
So since they're not gonna
university or work anymore,

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:24.280
they're just here all day long.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:25.590
And we're all really good friends.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.073
So we distract each other a lot.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.773
- He's filming. Jump on Apex.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.910
- [Joey] Yeah, I'm filming,
you guys can play Apex.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:35.883
I can't play Apex right now.

00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:38.443
- We've never played Apex
just us three before.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:41.910
- Thankfully though, over
the past week and a bit

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.248
I've really started to
ramp up the productivity.

00:01:44.248 --> 00:01:47.820
I'm starting to feel more
optimistic, like myself again,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.210
I'm kind of getting used to
this whole quarantine thing.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.890
So I wanted to share with you guys

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.950
some of the things that
have really helped me

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.620
climb out of this kind of
quarantine-induced, chaotic,

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:58.460
what am I even saying?

00:01:58.460 --> 00:01:59.940
I got out of a rut basically.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:01.860
Okay, so the first
thing I want to touch on

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.200
is that you need to be
aware of the yo-yo effect.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.440
So let me know if you've
ever been in this situation.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.050
You're in a little bit of a rut.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.930
You're playing more video games.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.730
You're not really taking care of yourself.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.940
You're just generally kind of slothful.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.660
You've lost your motivation
to be productive.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.400
And you kind of divulge
into the self-destruction

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.250
and pleasure seeking mindset

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.170
for a long enough period of time,

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.330
until you reach a breaking point

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:30.570
where you're like, wow, I really
feel like a piece of crap.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.700
I'm gonna make massive change.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.210
I'm gonna take massive action.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.430
So you quickly do a little 360,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.590
not 360, because if you did
a 360, we go in the same way

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.680
but you do a 180 and you start
going the other direction

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.560
and you start being super productive,

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.423
almost unnaturally productive.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.650
You're starting to hit the gym.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:51.970
You're moisturizing your face,

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.890
even though you never did that before.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.640
You're clipping your nails. Shower...

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.800
Why is everything I talk
about to do with grooming?

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.360
You're getting your work done on time.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.830
You're just insanely productive

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.770
and you're starting to feel really good.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.460
But you have a feeling
in the back of your mind

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.750
that it's unsustainable.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.650
And you eventually push yourself so hard.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.810
You get burnt out, you
lose all of your energy.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.160
So you yo-yo back in the other direction.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.730
And the cycle repeats itself.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.010
So if you've ever succumbed
to the yo-yo effect,

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.660
I'd imagine if you're a human being,

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.550
you have at some point in your life,

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.176
there's a couple reasons why this happens.

00:03:21.176 --> 00:03:23.970
First of all, you didn't
do the productive things

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.257
long enough for them to
be locked in as a habit.

00:03:26.257 --> 00:03:28.750
And the word habit's
kind of lost its meaning.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.040
But I mean something that is easier to do

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.370
than it is not to do.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.740
Once something is a habit,

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.470
you don't really have to worry
about yo-yoing back as much,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.980
because it's kind of
locked in psychologically.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.532
The second reason why we yo-yo

00:03:41.532 --> 00:03:44.670
is because the habits that we try to adopt

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.550
into our new lifestyle are so extreme,

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.480
we bite off more than
we can chew basically.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.250
So it's really important to
pick one or two easy things

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.420
that are simple to do,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.330
that you can actually count
on yourself to achieve daily,

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.060
lock these things in a simple daily habits

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.580
until they're easier to do than not to do.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:06.230
That way you won't get
burnt out by doing them.

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.670
Okay, so what does this have
to do with the quarantine?

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.040
Well, that's my next point.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.870
The morning is the key.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.220
One thing I've noticed,
especially during this quarantine,

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:18.900
is that your morning shapes
the entire rest of your day.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.380
In a pre-pandemic society,
people usually wake up,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.330
they shower, they get dressed,
pop something in their mouth,

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:29.580
maybe grab a coffee because
they have somewhere to be.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.060
They get out the door
to go to work or school.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:33.600
And that's their reason

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.790
for kind of taking their morning seriously

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.850
or getting into battle mode.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.300
But during the quarantine,

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.270
it's sort of hard to flip
that switch into battle mode,

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.350
because you're kind of
just staying at home.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.390
And even if you are working from home,

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.690
most people don't see the necessity

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.070
to treat working from home

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.370
the same as they would
leaving to go to work.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.070
But it is extremely important to do so.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.110
Whenever you shower and get dressed,

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.200
you kind of flip a switch in your brain

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.690
that says, I'm in work mode
now, or school mode now,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.740
or at least I'm ready to start the day.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.480
And if you wait to shower

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.790
and you wait till later
in the day to get dressed,

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.340
or you never get dressed,

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.510
you're either a postponing
flipping the switch

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:15.680
or not flipping the switch in general.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:17.750
For me, flipping the switch comes down

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.330
to a very simple decision
that I have to make

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.480
when I first wake up:

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.390
I can either choose the door on the left,

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.610
which leads to outside my room

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:26.890
and going through that door

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.120
always leads to me going down the stairs,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.900
making coffee and using
memes for half an hour

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.700
or the first hour of the day,

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.320
checking social media, et cetera.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.660
Or I can choose the second door

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.730
which is the door on my right,
which leads into my bathroom,

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.610
which always leads to me taking a shower,

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:46.090
putting clothes on, and
then walking downstairs.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:47.700
It's a very simple decision

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.570
but mentally I'm like two
completely different people

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.060
when I shower versus when I don't shower,

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.250
when I'm in sweat pants

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.390
versus when I actually
got ready for the day.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.570
Sweatpants me likes to browse memes

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.830
and check social media and binge YouTube.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.160
Showered me likes to light a candle

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.020
and write some content for YouTube.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.540
So find out what your little decision is

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.610
that you have to make every single morning

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.150
and flip that switch as early as possible.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.920
The third point I want to
make is to leverage boredom.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.240
So the quarantine is really boring.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.250
Spending all day every single day inside

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.190
is kind of a bummer.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:25.100
You start to run out of
stuff to do pretty quickly.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.770
But something I'd never
talked about with you guys

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.520
on this channel is that
this channel only exists

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:31.570
because of boredom.

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.530
And I only continued to
make videos early on,

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.670
especially when I wasn't
getting any views,

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.320
because of boredom.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.790
I asked my mom like, "Hey,
can I invite my friends over?"

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.053
And once we played Halo
Co-op on legendary too much,

00:06:46.053 --> 00:06:48.940
we got bored of that,
we went made a movie.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.770
There, I don't think I'd
be making videos today,

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:54.460
if I wasn't bored back
then with my friends.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.740
I was still in university and
between classes and studying

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.890
I didn't have an Xbox or a PC

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.980
capable of running very many games.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.800
In fact, I used a Chromebook

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.450
for the majority of my university career,

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.650
and I was itching to find
something to do with my time,

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.570
something to work on or work towards.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.700
And that's why I started this channel.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.910
When we fill every single parcel of time

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.070
with something to do
with a screen to look at

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:26.590
or a show to watch, we almost
lose touch with ourselves.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:30.750
But this aloneness or this
boredom is extremely important

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.550
because it helps you uncover things

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:34.960
that you need to address.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.410
It can either lead you
to think about and accept

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:41.030
something traumatizing that
happened to you in the past,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.040
or sometimes it's not that scary,

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.940
and you just have this underlying anxiety

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.770
because you haven't paid your taxes yet.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.620
All these emails and letters
are starting to pile up,

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:51.700
and this anxiety is the catalyst

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.740
for taking action on those things.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.260
But if you keep distracting yourself,

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.287
you're never gonna have that,

00:07:56.287 --> 00:07:59.360
you're never gonna be
scared into taking action.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.360
You know, that boredom created
sort of like this itch to,

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:08.830
you know, go do something to to fill time.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:12.828
Instead of just like having
your time filled for you

00:08:12.828 --> 00:08:14.070
via social media.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.530
So, you're gonna have a lot
of time during this quarantine

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.970
to sit alone with your
thoughts and try to figure out

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.730
what the most important
thing in your life is.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.220
Don't distract yourself from this feeling.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.190
So the last point I want to talk about

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.210
is probably the most important,

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.340
and that's this idea
of using the quarantine

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:33.730
to your advantage.

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:37.080
It's very easy to take on a victim role.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.810
In fact, it makes a lot of sense.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:41.410
There's a compelling
reason to play the victim

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:42.410
during a pandemic,

00:08:42.410 --> 00:08:44.540
because you can't really
do anything about it.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.220
It feels out of your control.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.920
It's scary. It's untreaded territory.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:50.440
There's tons of reasons

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.330
why you can fall into feeling helpless

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:58.160
or confused or lose your
way in some form or another.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.200
It's a lot harder, but probably
a lot more advantageous

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:03.940
to view it as an opportunity,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.650
because the nature of being
on house arrest for 24/7

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:10.890
actually has a huge advantage.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.140
And that's because you have

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:15.010
a tremendous amount of predictability

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:16.700
with what your day looks like.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.570
And that is the breeding ground

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.660
for developing healthy habits.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.633
If every day feels like Groundhog day,

00:09:22.633 --> 00:09:25.280
then that's the perfect opportunity,

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.080
this is fertile soil
for developing habits.

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.200
A lot of people can't start healthy habits

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.920
during their regular life
because you know, things pop up,

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.860
you know, maybe you have
to go away for work.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:39.240
Little Johnny has a baseball
game you have to go watch.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.400
So you have to skip the gym.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:41.920
There's all these little things

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:43.920
that regular life throws at you.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.090
Quarantine life is a lot more boring,

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:49.670
a lot more monotonous, but
a lot more predictable.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.490
So this is probably the best
opportunity of your life

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.170
to lock in habits

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.570
that you can then take
into your normal life,

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.290
once this whole thing blows over.

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.430
And if you're someone who
has generally struggled

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.510
to implement good habits,

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:05.450
if you're somebody who's tried to meditate

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:07.810
for a day or two or three,

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:09.170
and then it kind of falls off,

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.330
or you try to develop a habit of reading

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:12.750
and then that kind of falls off,

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:15.050
then I'm actually gonna
re recommend a book

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.430
that I've already
recommended on this channel,

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:19.290
but I think it's the most important book

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.060
you can read at a time like this.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.710
And that is "Atomic
Habits" by James Clear.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.881
I'm rereading it myself and
I'm uncovering so many things

00:10:26.881 --> 00:10:29.090
that I somehow missed before.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.070
And it's making me realize
it's one of those books

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:34.000
that you should read several times.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.610
And I highly recommend checking
out the audio book version,

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:39.263
specifically using today's
video sponsor Audible.

00:10:39.263 --> 00:10:41.640
And that is because it's actually narrated

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.050
by the author himself.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:46.410
So his personal anecdotes that
he wrote about in the book

00:10:46.410 --> 00:10:48.520
really hit home and feel more personal.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.090
Not only does audible
have the largest selection

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.150
of audio books on the entire internet,

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.720
but every single month
they send you one credit,

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:58.040
which you can spend on any audio book

00:10:58.040 --> 00:10:59.560
in their entire library.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.230
And they also send you
